D.add_heading('Structured Article Summary',1)
D.add_heading('Background and Aim',2)
para(D,'Mitral transcatheter edge-to-edge repair (M-TEER) is unusually dependent on echocardiographic navigation because device position, orientation, leaflet interaction, residual mitral regurgitation (MR), and transmitral obstruction must be inferred rather than directly seen. The 2026 American Society of Echocardiography guideline formalizes step-specific use of two-dimensional, biplane, three-dimensional (3D), and multiplanar-reconstruction imaging while explicitly noting that no standard algorithm currently directs the interventional echocardiographer [1]. We developed a literature-parameterized information-guided view-selection framework that treats intraprocedural imaging as a sequential allocation problem: at each step the model asks which view offers the largest clinically weighted reduction in uncertainty about coaptation gap, coaptation length, residual 3D-VCA, and transmitral mean gradient for the least acquisition burden. The aim was not to predict patient outcomes, but to establish a mathematically testable architecture for future retrospective calibration using time-stamped M-TEER imaging.')
D.add_heading('Methods',2)
para(D,'A four-dimensional latent state x=[g,c,r,m] represented coaptation gap g (mm), coaptation length c (mm), residual 3D-VCA r (cm²), and transmitral mean gradient m (mmHg). Literature supplied clinical anchors—historical EVEREST geometry, leaflet length, residual-VCA strata, and the commonly used 5-mmHg gradient boundary—while prior uncertainty, observation-noise matrices, acquisition penalties, and utility weights remained provisional. Seven candidate acquisitions were represented by view-specific observation matrices: intercommissural, LVOT long-axis, biplane X-plane, 3D en-face, 3D MPR, 3D color VCA, and continuous-wave Doppler. Gaussian belief states were propagated with process perturbation and updated analytically by a Kalman-form posterior covariance recursion; expected information gain was computed from multivariate differential entropy. A phase-aware utility weighted entropy reduction, clinically prioritized variance reduction, procedural phase, and acquisition penalty. Four latent procedural regimes—targeting, alignment, grasping, and result assessment—modified view utility. A finite-horizon Bellman interpretation and uncertainty-based optimal-stopping guardrail defined when further imaging ceased to add sufficient model value. Mathematical analysis examined covariance positivity, boundedness, information monotonicity after observation, stationary covariance under repeated acquisition cycles, and the spectral radius of a finite-difference Jacobian of the Riccati-type covariance map. Computational experiments comprised a reference sequence, 600 literature-consistent synthetic truth scenarios, five policy comparators, 1,200 model-generated action states for internal AIC/BIC recovery, 500 Latin-hypercube parameter sets with PRCC, ±1% local elasticity, eight prespecified structural scenarios, and a two-dimensional uncertainty sensitivity surface. No Runge–Kutta, finite-difference PDE solver, or finite-element method was used because the proposed problem contains neither a continuous-time ODE requiring numerical integration nor a spatial PDE; adding such solvers would be mathematically decorative rather than justified.')
D.add_heading('Results',2)
para(D,'In the reference proof-of-concept, the phase-aware policy selected 3D MPR first, followed by 3D color VCA and CW Doppler, reaching the prespecified uncertainty guardrail after three acquisitions. Cumulative information gain was 5.432 nats with an acquisition-cost sum of 3.95 arbitrary units (1.375 nats per cost unit). Posterior SD fell from 2.60 to 0.520 mm for coaptation gap, from 1.40 to 0.459 mm for coaptation length, from 0.140 to 0.0489 cm² for residual 3D-VCA, and from 1.50 to 0.384 mmHg for gradient; these are uncertainty reductions within the assumed observation model, not measured echocardiographic precision. Across 600 synthetic scenarios, the phase-aware policy used a median of 3 views and met the model stopping rule in 600/600 runs, whereas a prespecified fixed six-view sequence required a median of 6 acquisitions and an unweighted variance-greedy policy exhausted the 8-view horizon without meeting the multidomain stopping rule. The entropy-only policy also stopped after 3 views, demonstrating that the current proof-of-concept does not establish superiority over every adaptive alternative. In the separate 1,200-state internal recovery experiment, however, the full phase-aware representation best recovered the mechanism used to generate stochastic action labels (AIC 715.0, BIC 760.8, internal action accuracy 0.910) compared with entropy-only (AIC 4293.4; accuracy 0.737), geometry-weighted (AIC 4580.6; accuracy 0.736), clinical weights without phase (AIC 9736.5; accuracy 0.201), and cost-only (AIC 10751.3; accuracy 0.113); these are model-recovery metrics, not clinical performance. Global sensitivity of terminal entropy was dominated by the coaptation-length stopping tolerance (PRCC 0.561, p<10^-40), followed by gap tolerance (PRCC 0.287, p<10^-10) and VCA tolerance (PRCC 0.250, p<10^-7). Repeated single-view covariance maps retained near-unit spectral radii because each view leaves some states incompletely observed, whereas the complementary 3D-MPR→3D-color-VCA→CW-Doppler cycle had a spectral radius of 0.571, supporting contraction of the full uncertainty state under the model assumptions.')
D.add_heading('Conclusions',2)
para(D,'The framework provides a mechanistically interpretable proof-of-concept for a practical problem that static image-quality scores do not directly address: which echocardiographic acquisition should come next when several clinically relevant quantities remain uncertain during M-TEER. Its intended use is to organize time-stamped imaging into an auditable decision process, quantify the marginal information value of additional views, and specify the minimum variables required for later clinical calibration. The potential clinical benefit is not automated procedural control but more explicit communication about what remains uncertain—geometry, residual MR, or hemodynamics—before device release or further intervention. The research advantage is modular falsifiability: the observation matrices, noise terms, phase weights, stopping thresholds, and utilities can each be recalibrated or rejected using retrospective or prospective M-TEER datasets. Parameter refinement and biological/technical calibration are ongoing; external validation is required before any patient-level recommendation can be inferred.')

add_fig(D,'Figure_1_Model_Architecture.png','Figure 1. Hybrid information-guided architecture linking literature anchors, latent anatomical/hemodynamic states, Bayesian belief updating, phase-aware view allocation, and stopping decisions.')

D.add_heading('1. INTRODUCTION',1)
para(D,'M-TEER is an image-mediated intervention: successful navigation depends on transforming multiple acoustic views into a coherent estimate of leaflet geometry, device alignment, grasping adequacy, residual MR, and post-repair hemodynamics. The contemporary imaging literature describes why each of these quantities matters. Historical EVEREST criteria placed coaptation length, coaptation depth, flail gap, flail width, and mitral valve area at the center of anatomic feasibility [2–5]; contemporary procedural reviews describe intercommissural and LVOT long-axis views, X-plane imaging, 3D en-face reconstruction, and 3D MPR as complementary rather than interchangeable sources of information [6–8]. Recent clinical evidence further strengthens the choice of model states: a 2,140-patient functional-MR cohort identified a visible coaptation gap in 10% of cases [9]; a 2026 3D-TEE study in atrial functional MR showed that leaflet-to-annulus geometry was associated with residual MR, device count, and post-TEER gradient [10]; MITRA-PRO linked intraprocedural residual 3D-VCA to subsequent outcome gradients [11]; and large registries and trials show that residual MR and transmitral gradient jointly define clinically meaningful post-TEER hemodynamic states [12–17]. These findings create a decision-science problem rather than a conventional prediction problem. The interventional echocardiographer does not merely need another static score; the unresolved question is how to allocate the next acquisition when uncertainty is distributed unevenly across anatomy and hemodynamics. The 2026 ASE guideline is particularly timely because it specifies required imaging content but states that no standard algorithm currently directs a successful M-TEER procedure [1]. We therefore formulated intraprocedural imaging as a partially observed sequential decision process with an explicit value-of-information interpretation drawn from financial engineering: acquiring another view is analogous to retaining an information option whose value must exceed its acquisition burden. Unlike a financial asset analogy, however, clinical weights are attached to uncertainty in anatomically and hemodynamically meaningful variables, and the model is deliberately constrained to decision support rather than autonomous control.')

D.add_heading('2. METHODS',1)
para(D,'The study was designed as a deterministic–stochastic computational proof-of-concept in which clinical literature constrains state definitions and anchor values while the mathematical coefficients governing epistemic uncertainty remain explicitly provisional. The latent vector x_t=[g_t,c_t,r_t,m_t] contains coaptation gap, coaptation length, residual 3D-VCA, and transmitral mean gradient. Historical coaptation-length ≥2 mm, coaptation-depth <11 mm, flail-gap <10 mm, flail-width <15 mm, leaflet-length around 10 mm, valve-area around 4 cm², residual-VCA strata at 0.10 and 0.30 cm², and the commonly analyzed 5-mmHg post-TEER gradient were stored as clinical anchors rather than fitted parameters [2–5,11–17]. The belief state b_t=N(μ_t,P_t) represents what the imaging team currently knows and, crucially, what remains uncertain. Each candidate view v has an observation matrix H_v specifying which latent variables it informs and a covariance R_v representing provisional measurement uncertainty. Intercommissural and LVOT views primarily inform geometry; biplane X-plane, 3D en-face, and 3D MPR jointly inform gap and coaptation; 3D color-VCA informs residual MR; and CW Doppler informs transmitral gradient, consistent with procedural imaging literature [1,6–8]. Between acquisitions, a small process covariance Q allows physiological, device, and image-state perturbation. The posterior covariance update is a Riccati-type contraction, and expected information gain is the decrease in multivariate Gaussian entropy. To prevent a purely mathematical preference for any view with high variance reduction, the acquisition utility combines entropy gain, clinically weighted normalized SD reduction, phase-specific relevance, and a dimensionless cost penalty. Four latent procedural regimes—targeting, alignment, grasping, and result assessment—were defined to approximate the stepwise clinical workflow described in current guidance [1,6–8]. The policy π(b,z) selects the action maximizing expected value; stopping occurs only after at least three acquisitions when all four posterior SDs satisfy provisional tolerances of 0.65 mm, 0.50 mm, 0.055 cm², and 0.55 mmHg. These tolerances are computational guardrails, not recommended echocardiographic precision targets. Internal mathematical consistency was assessed by positive-semidefinite covariance checks, log-determinant validity, bounded SD trajectories, and contraction analysis. Repeated-view and complementary-cycle covariance maps were iterated to stationary states, and finite-difference Jacobians on the ten independent covariance elements were used to calculate spectral radii. Computational robustness included 600 synthetic truth scenarios drawn from broad literature-consistent ranges but explicitly not treated as patients; five decision policies (fixed sequence, variance-greedy, entropy-only, clinical weighting without phase, and full phase-aware); an internal softmax action-recovery experiment on 1,200 model-generated states compared using AIC and BIC; 500 Latin-hypercube parameter sets; PRCC after ranked residualization; ±1% local elasticity; eight structural scenarios; and a first-view sensitivity surface across coaptation-gap and residual-VCA uncertainty. The random seed was 20260903. Because no raw clinical imaging was analyzed, no ROC, calibration curve, sensitivity, specificity, hazard ratio, or clinical p value is reported for the proposed policy.')

D.add_heading('Mathematical Model Equations',2)
EQS=[
('Latent anatomical–hemodynamic state','x_t = [g_t, c_t, r_t, m_t]^T'),
('Gaussian belief state','b_t = N(μ_t, P_t)'),
('Prediction covariance','P_t^- = P_{t-1} + Q'),
('View-specific observation model','y_t(v) = H_v x_t + ε_v,   ε_v ~ N(0, R_v)'),
('Kalman information gain matrix','K_t = P_t^- H_v^T [H_v P_t^- H_v^T + R_v]^{-1}'),
('Posterior state mean','μ_t = μ_t^- + K_t [y_t − H_v μ_t^-]'),
('Posterior covariance','P_t = [I − K_t H_v] P_t^-'),
('Multivariate differential entropy','H(P_t) = 1/2 {d[1 + log(2π)] + log det(P_t)}'),
('Expected information gain','IG(v|b_t) = H(P_t^-) − H(P_t(v))'),
('Clinically weighted uncertainty reduction','ΔU_v = Σ_j w_j [sqrt(P^-_{jj})/s_j − sqrt(P_{jj}(v))/s_j]'),
('Phase-conditioned acquisition utility','A(v|b_t,z_t) = φ_v(z_t)[0.58 IG(v|b_t) + 0.42 ΔU_v] − λ c_v'),
('Sequential view policy','π*(b_t,z_t) = argmax_v A(v|b_t,z_t)'),
('Bellman decision representation','V_t(b,z) = max{U_stop(b,z), max_v[-λc_v + E{V_{t+1}(b^v,z^v)}]}'),
('Information stopping time','τ* = inf{t ≥ 3 : sqrt(P_{jj,t}) ≤ δ_j for all j}'),
('Latent procedural regime','z_t ∈ {Targeting, Alignment, Grasping, Result assessment}'),
('State-transition law','Pr(z_{t+1}=k | z_t=j,b_t,v_t) = T_{jk}(b_t,v_t)'),
('Residual-MR uncertainty hazard','h_R(t) = h_0 exp{β_g σ_g(t) + β_c σ_c(t) + β_r σ_r(t) + β_m σ_m(t)}'),
('Riccati covariance map','F_v(P) = P+Q − (P+Q)H_v^T[H_v(P+Q)H_v^T+R_v]^{-1}H_v(P+Q)'),
('Complementary-cycle stability criterion','ρ{D(F_{v_k}∘…∘F_{v_1})(P*)} < 1'),
('Akaike information criterion','AIC = 2k − 2 log L'),
('Bayesian information criterion','BIC = k log n − 2 log L'),
('Partial rank correlation','PRCC(θ_j,Y) = Corr(rank-residual θ_j, rank-residual Y)'),
('Local elasticity','E_{θ,Y} = (ΔY/Y)/(Δθ/θ)')]
for a,b in EQS: eq(D,a,b)

