D.add_heading('3. RESULTS',1)
para(D,'The current framework establishes the mathematical structure underlying information-guided intraprocedural view selection. Preliminary theoretical analyses indicate that covariance remained positive semidefinite throughout all tested sequences and that each observation reduced entropy relative to its corresponding predicted covariance, as required by the linear-Gaussian update. Initial simulations suggest that a complementary sequence is more important than repeatedly sampling a single informative modality: in the reference scenario, 3D MPR was selected first during targeting, reducing the normalized coaptation-gap SD from 1.000 to 0.189 and coaptation-length SD from 1.000 to 0.318 while contributing 2.868 nats of information gain; 3D color VCA followed, contributing 1.309 nats and reducing residual-VCA SD from 0.140 to 0.0746 cm²; CW Doppler then contributed 1.255 nats and reduced gradient SD from 1.50 to 0.384 mmHg. The three-step sequence accumulated 5.432 nats at a total acquisition penalty of 3.95 model units. At stopping, posterior SDs were 0.520 mm for coaptation gap, 0.459 mm for coaptation length, 0.0489 cm² for VCA, and 0.384 mmHg for gradient; the posterior means in the reference synthetic state were 6.02 mm, 3.43 mm, 0.285 cm², and 3.47 mmHg, respectively, and must not be interpreted as patient measurements. Across 600 stress-test scenarios, both the phase-aware and entropy-only policies reached their guardrails with a median of 3 acquisitions; the fixed clinically plausible sequence required 6, while variance-greedy selection repeatedly revisited geometry/hemodynamic views and failed the four-domain guardrail by the 8-view horizon. Thus, the framework demonstrates efficiency relative to a fixed sequence but does not yet demonstrate phase-aware superiority over entropy-only selection. The internal action-recovery experiment addressed a different question—whether simplified statistical representations can recover the action mechanism used to generate stochastic choices. The phase-aware model achieved AIC 715.0 and BIC 760.8 with 91.0% internal action agreement, versus AIC 4293.4 for entropy-only, 4580.6 for geometry-weighted, 9736.5 for clinical weighting without phase, and 10751.3 for cost-only. Because labels were generated by the full model, this experiment is a structural recovery check rather than external validation. In 500 Latin-hypercube runs, coaptation-length stopping tolerance had the strongest PRCC with terminal entropy (0.561), followed by gap tolerance (0.287) and VCA tolerance (0.250); acquisition cost and process-noise scale were smaller in this reference range. Single-view repeated maps had spectral radii approximately 1 because unobserved state dimensions preserve uncertainty, while the complementary MPR–VCA–Doppler cycle had spectral radius 0.571, indicating contraction of the full covariance state under repeated complementary acquisition. The sensitivity surface further showed that the preferred first view changed when the initial uncertainty allocation shifted, supporting the conceptual premise that view choice should depend on what is unknown rather than on a universal fixed order. Parameter refinement and technical calibration are ongoing.')

