add_fig(D,'Figure_5_Global_Sensitivity_PRCC.png','Figure 5. PRCC sensitivity of terminal posterior entropy to provisional structural and stopping parameters.')
add_fig(D,'Figure_6_View_Selection_Surface.png','Figure 6. First-view decision surface as initial coaptation-gap and residual-VCA uncertainty vary.')
add_fig(D,'Figure_7_Covariance_Stability.png','Figure 7. Spectral radius of repeated complementary covariance-update cycles. The full complementary cycle is contractive under reference assumptions.')
add_fig(D,'Table_3_Model_Comparison.png','Table 3 rendered as a figure. Internal model-recovery results; the apparent advantage of the full model reflects recovery of a full-model data-generating mechanism.')
add_fig(D,'Table_4_Policy_Summary.png','Table 4 rendered as a figure. Synthetic policy stress tests; scenarios are not patients.')
add_fig(D,'Table_5_Scenario_Results.png','Table 5 rendered as a figure. Prespecified structural sensitivity scenarios.')

D.add_heading('4. DISCUSSION',1)
para(D,'This proof-of-concept reframes a familiar clinical workflow as an information-allocation problem. The practical difficulty in M-TEER is rarely that the imager does not know any useful views; rather, several views are available and their marginal usefulness changes as the procedure progresses. Current guidance already recognizes this complementarity: intercommissural and long-axis planes help trajectory and grasping, 3D MPR improves anatomical reconstruction, color Doppler and 3D-VCA interrogate residual MR, and CW Doppler interrogates transmitral obstruction [1,6–8]. The model formalizes that logic by maintaining a belief state and assigning an explicit information value to the next acquisition. This is where financial-engineering reasoning contributes without forcing financial terminology into the clinical title: the imaging team holds an option to acquire more information, but that option has a cost in procedural time, cognitive load, and potentially repeated manipulation; stopping is justified only when the marginal value of further information is insufficient under the specified guardrails. The resulting framework also captures a clinically important asymmetry that static association models may obscure. Coaptation geometry is essential early, but after adequate geometric certainty is reached, repeatedly refining the same geometry may be less valuable than resolving residual MR or gradient uncertainty. The complementary-cycle stability result makes this intuition mathematically explicit: no single view fully contracts the four-dimensional uncertainty state, whereas repeated complementary sampling can. The model is therefore closer to a partially observed decision process than to a conventional risk score. Its clinical value, if later validated, would be as a transparent layer over existing imaging practice: the system would report which domain is driving uncertainty and why another view is being suggested, leaving acquisition and device decisions with the Heart Team. The 2026 ASE statement that no standard directing algorithm exists [1] makes this a timely target for collaborative validation rather than evidence that a new algorithm is already clinically ready.')

D.add_heading('5. LIMITATIONS',1)
para(D,'The principal limitation is deliberate: this is not a patient-validated model. Observation matrices and measurement-noise covariances are provisional and were not estimated from paired expert acquisitions, so the posterior SDs cannot be interpreted as real-world echocardiographic precision. The latent state compresses a much richer procedure into four variables and does not yet include leaflet insertion length, clip orientation angle, annular area, TLA/MAA, pulmonary-vein flow, left-atrial pressure, heart rate, loading conditions, device generation, or MR mechanism; these variables can be added after identifiability analysis. The Gaussian observation assumption is convenient but may be inadequate for multimodal, artifact-prone images. The phase rules are simplified representations of procedural workflow and are not learned from time-stamped cases. The internal AIC/BIC experiment uses model-generated labels and therefore cannot establish generalizability, discrimination, calibration, or clinical benefit. The 600 synthetic scenarios and 500 Latin-hypercube sets quantify sensitivity to assumptions rather than interpatient epidemiology. Historical EVEREST geometry thresholds remain useful anchors but should not be interpreted as contemporary absolute eligibility criteria. The current formulation also uses an abstract acquisition penalty rather than measured seconds, probe manipulation burden, or operator cognitive load. No causal effect of a suggested view on MR reduction, procedure time, complication rate, or outcomes is claimed. Finally, the framework does not attempt a biological equilibrium, phase-plane disease model, FEM, or numerical ODE integration because those methods do not match the mathematical object studied here; the relevant stability problem is the evolution of posterior covariance and decision state. These limitations define the next research phase: retrospective extraction of time-stamped view sequences, paired expert annotations, estimation of view-specific measurement distributions, structural identifiability checks, temporal cross-validation by center/operator/device, and prospective silent-mode evaluation before any interventional use.')

D.add_heading('6. CONCLUSION',1)
para(D,'This mechanistic computational proof-of-concept provides a mathematically testable framework for identifying the next informative intraprocedural echocardiographic acquisition during M-TEER—an uncertainty-allocation problem that conventional association-based analyses do not directly reveal. Its practical usage is to convert coaptation geometry, residual MR, and transmitral hemodynamic uncertainty into an explicit belief state and to rank candidate views by their expected marginal information value. Its potential benefit is a more transparent and reproducible way to identify whether the unresolved procedural question is anatomical, regurgitant, or hemodynamic before additional device manipulation or procedural completion. Its research advantage is that every component is auditable and replaceable: observation matrices can be learned from paired images, phase transitions from time-stamped workflows, costs from procedural timing, and stopping tolerances from expert consensus or outcome-linked calibration. The framework is designed to guide variable selection, protocol development, and subsequent retrospective or prospective validation in collaboration with structural heart imaging and interventional teams. Parameter refinement and technical calibration are ongoing. Clinical calibration and external validation are planned using retrospective or prospective M-TEER cohorts. Final effect estimates must be recalculated from the underlying registry data before scientific or clinical interpretation.')

D.add_heading('7. DATA AND CODE AVAILABILITY',1)
para(D,'All data distributed with this work are either literature-source ledgers or model-generated outputs. The reproducibility package includes the Python model, figure-generation code, manuscript-generation code, reference acquisition sequence, view-utility matrix, 600-scenario policy stress test, 1,200-state synthetic model-recovery comparison, 500-sample Latin-hypercube analysis, PRCC sensitivity results, local elasticity results, covariance-equilibrium and stability analyses, structural scenarios, two-dimensional sensitivity surface, model-parameter ledger, literature-source ledger, equation list, and machine-readable analysis summary. Because no patient-level dataset or raw echocardiographic image was analyzed, no clinical dataset is available for distribution. The package is intended to regenerate every theoretical number and figure exactly and to make later replacement of provisional assumptions with observed longitudinal imaging estimates transparent.')

